[1mThe library[22m is tiny: [38;5;10mc_src[0m is two files exposing a single function, [38;5;10mvoid md5_digest(const tflac_md5 *m, tflac_u8 out[16])[0m, which serializes four [38;5;10muint32_t[0m words as 16 little-endian bytes.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mThe bug — aliasing fidelity.[22m The C body is sixteen separate statements, each re-reading [38;5;10mm->a[0m/[38;5;10mm->b[0m/… before storing one byte. Since [38;5;10mout[0m is [38;5;10mtflac_u8 *[0m (i.e. [38;5;10munsigned char *[0m), it is exempt from strict aliasing, so a store through [38;5;10mout[0m can legally modify [38;5;10m*m[0m and the compiler may not cache the word. When [38;5;10mout[0m partially overlaps [38;5;10mm[0m, each store feeds the next load and the result is a byte-by-byte cascade. The original Rust read each word once and wrote it with a 4-byte [38;5;10mcopy_from_slice[0m:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mimage:  2c 8b b9 c8 c1 9d 61 74 26 43 85 ce f6 16 e9 7a   (out = m + 1)
C   :   2c 2c 2c 2c 2c 2c 2c 2c 2c 2c 2c 2c 2c 2c 2c 2c 2c
Rust:   2c 2c 8b b9 c8 c8 9d 61 74 74 43 85 ce ce 16 e9 7a
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Fixed in [38;5;10msrc/lib.rs[0m by re-loading the source word immediately before each of the sixteen stores, with volatile byte-granularity loads and volatile stores so the optimizer cannot re-cache, widen, or reorder them. I confirmed this is standard-mandated semantics rather than an unoptimized-build artifact by rebuilding the C at [38;5;10m-O0/-O1/-O2/-O3/-Os[0m into a temp directory ([38;5;10mc_src/[0m untouched, md5sums verified) — the Rust matches all five.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Note that [3mfull[23m aliasing ([38;5;10mout == m[0m) is a fixed point and cannot detect this; only the partial-overlap cases can, which is why the plain happy path looked fine.[0m[0m
